  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nWe adopt a multi-phase optimization strategy combining structured initializations, adaptive sampling, and hybrid solvers to escape local optima. The core idea is to leverage both structured patterns (like optimized periodic functions) and random perturbations to explore the solution space more effectively. By alternating between gradient-based optimization and population-based methods, we balance computational efficiency with global search capability. This approach directly targets the nonconvex nature of the problem by using problem-specific constraints and adaptive regularization.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nVariables: \n- h \u2208 [0,1]^n_points with \u2211h = n_points / 2\n- Objective: minimize c5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k))dx = max_k (dx * corr_k) where corr_k = \u2211h[i](1-h[i+k]) for valid i+k\n- Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1, \u2211h[i] = n_points/2\n\nImplementation:\n- Use convex feasibility checks to ensure constraints are satisfied\n- Employ Lipschitz-continuous surrogates for nonconvex terms\n- Maintain dual representations (discrete and continuous) for validation\n- Use exact correlation computation with proper scaling (dx = 2/n_points)\n\n3. Implementation procedure\nStage 1: Parent validation\n- Check if initial_h_values is valid (sum \u2248 n_points/2, entries \u2208 [0,1])\n- If provided, use as base with 5% random perturbation\n- Otherwise, generate a base pattern via Fourier series: h[i] = 0.5 + 0.25*sin(\u03c0i/n_points)\n\nStage 2: Initialization\n- Generate 4 distinct initial patterns:\n  1. Base pattern from Stage 1\n  2. Optimized LP relaxation (constrained h[i] = 0.5 + \u03b5*sin(\u03c0i/n_points))\n  3. Random binary vector with \u2211h = n_points/2\n  4. Structured pattern with 5% density of 1s and 1s at symmetric points\n\nStage 3: Core optimization\n- For each initial pattern:\n  1. Run L-BFGS-B with bound constraints (0,1) and equality constraint \u2211h = n_points/2\n  2. Switch to SLSQP for 200 iterations with additional constraints:\n     - \u2211h = n_points/2\n     - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n     - Lipshitz-continuous regularizer: \u2211|h[i] - h[i-1]| \u2264 0.5\n  3. Use early-stopping if objective improves by <1e-5 over 50 iterations\n  4. Apply feasibility repair: clip values and adjust sum with linear interpolation\n\nStage 4: Global search\n- For top 3 candidates from Stage 3:\n  1. Apply differential evolution with 100 agents\n  2. Use bounds [0,1] and maintain diversity through mutation\n  3. Hybridize with local optimization: run L-BFGS-B on best DE candidates\n  4. Use memory-based selection: retain top 50 candidates from all iterations\n\nStage 5: Final evaluation\n- All candidates are re-evaluated with exact correlation computation\n- Select the candidate with smallest c5 value\n- Verify constraints: \u2211h \u2248 n_points/2 (tolerance 1e-6), entries \u2208 [0,1]\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\nDefault configuration:\n- n_points = 400 (balances resolution and computation time)\n- Initial patterns: 4 variations as specified\n- Optimization phases: 300 L-BFGS-B iterations, 200 SLSQP iterations\n- DE parameters: population size 100, mutation factor 0.8, crossover probability 0.9\n- Regularization strength: 0.5 for Lipschitz constraint\n- Fallback: Use initial_h_values if no better candidate found\n\nVariations:\n- When initial_h_values is available: \n  - Use it as base with 3% random perturbation\n  - Increase DE population to 150 for better exploration\n- When n_points < 300: \n  - Reduce DE iterations to 150\n  - Use simpler regularizer (0.3 Lipschitz)\n- When c5 < 0.38: \n  - Increase resolution to 600 points\n  - Add penalty for high variance (\u03c3^2(h) \u2264 0.1)\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\nTime allocation:\n- Stage 1: 10s (validation and initial pattern generation)\n- Stage 2: 30s (initial pattern creation)\n- Stage 3: 200s (core optimization)\n- Stage 4: 200s (global search)\n- Stage 5: 30s (final evaluation)\n\nRobustness measures:\n- Time monitoring: Check elapsed time every 30s during optimization\n- Early stopping: Terminate optimization if no improvement in 100 iterations\n- Fallback: If time runs out, return the best candidate found\n- Numerical stability: Clip all values between 0 and 1 before final evaluation\n- Reconstruction: For new n_points, resample the best pattern with linear interpolation\n\n6. Validation and return contract\nFeasibility checks:\n- All returned h_values must satisfy 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\n- \u2211h \u2248 n_points/2 within 1e-6 tolerance\n- No NaN or infinite values in the returned array\n\nValidation procedure:\n1. Recompute \u2211h and verify against n_points/2\n2. Check all entries are within [0,1]\n3. Recompute the correlation objective with exact dx = 2/n_points\n4. Verify that the returned c5_bound matches the computed value\n\nSanity checks:\n- Check that \u2211h is exactly n_points/2 (within 1e-9)\n- Verify that the correlation array has length 2*n_points - 1\n- Confirm that the maximum value in the correlation array is properly scaled\n- Ensure that the returned h_values are not a copy of initial_h_values",
